  "subtitle": "Low-latency trading path",
  "intent": "Order matching is kept on a single-AZ EC2 instance beside an in-memory order book so the latency-critical path never crosses a zone boundary, while everything durable is replicated across zones behind it.",
  "reviewed": "2026-08-26",
  "annotations": [
    {
      "n": 1,
      "target": "route53",
      "text": "<b>Amazon Route 53</b> latency-based records send each client to the nearest entry point. <b>AWS Global Accelerator</b> sits behind it to hold a stable anycast IP, which matters for API clients that pin addresses and cannot follow a DNS change quickly."
    },
    {
      "n": 2,
      "target": "waf",
      "text": "<b>AWS WAF</b> and <b>AWS Shield</b> are attached at the edge rather than at the load balancer, so abusive traffic is dropped before it consumes matching-engine capacity."
    },
    {
      "n": 3,
      "target": "engine",
      "text": "The matching engine runs on <b>Amazon EC2</b> rather than a container service: the hot path needs pinned cores, placement-group locality and kernel tuning that a managed scheduler will not guarantee."
    },
    {
      "n": 4,
      "target": "redis",
      "text": "The live order book is held in <b>Amazon ElastiCache</b> in the same Availability Zone as the engine. A cross-zone hop costs more than the entire matching budget, so this pair is deliberately not spread for availability."
    },
    {
      "n": 5,
      "target": "msk",
      "text": "<b>Amazon MSK</b> decouples matching from settlement, reporting and market data. Consumers can be slow or restarted without applying back pressure to the trading path."
    },
    {
      "n": 6,
      "target": "data",
      "text": "Everything durable sits behind the hot path: <b>Amazon DynamoDB</b> for trade state, <b>Amazon Aurora</b> for accounts, <b>Amazon S3</b> for the archive. This layer is replicated across zones; the engine is not."
    }
  ]
}